# A name bound to what the language's own code computes of names, with no call, stands for those names (#328), and
# the body's own code reads those names where it reads the name, never where it binds it (#343): `named` binds the gap
# between the level and the module's quiet level (`first.db - QUIET.db`) and the array of both (`[first, &QUIET]`) to
# names before it hands each to a step, and `written` writes each where its step is handed it. An operator is no
# occurrence and a name adds no arrow, so the two routes are one plan: each step takes the level and the quiet level,
# which alone comes from beyond, and the route's own arrow reads neither where the body binds a name to them.

route signal.named
step named/draw
step named/list

line entry > named call
line Level·first > named value
line Level·first > named/draw value                  # `draw(gap)`, `let gap = first.db - QUIET.db`
line Level·QUIET > named/draw value                  # the module's quiet level, from beyond
line Level·first > named/list value                  # `list(&both)`, `let both = [first, &QUIET]`
line Level·QUIET > named/list value
line named/draw > Screen own
line named/list > Screen own

home Level·first named
home Level·QUIET named

side entry left
side Screen right

route signal.written
step written/draw
step written/list

line entry > written call
line Level·first > written value
line Level·first > written/draw value                # `draw(first.db - QUIET.db)`
line Level·QUIET > written/draw value
line Level·first > written/list value                # `list(&[first, &QUIET])`
line Level·QUIET > written/list value
line written/draw > Screen own
line written/list > Screen own

home Level·first written
home Level·QUIET written

side entry left
side Screen right

# A name a step changes in place holds a value of its own from its binding on, and stands for nothing (#355): `gathered`
# binds a vector of the first level (`vec![first]`) and pushes the second onto it. The body's own code makes the vector
# where it binds it, and reads the first level there; the push is handed that vector and the second level and makes the
# vector anew, and the step that lists it takes what the push made. No step takes the first level.

route signal.gathered
step gathered/push
step gathered/list

line entry > gathered call
line Level·first > gathered value                    # `let mut both = vec![first]`
line Level·second > gathered value
line Level·both@gathered/~both > gathered/push value # `both.push(second)`
line Level·second > gathered/push value
line gathered/push > Level·both@gathered/push value
line Level·both@gathered/push > gathered/list value  # `list(&both)`
line gathered/list > Screen own

home Level·first gathered
home Level·second gathered
home Level·both@gathered/~both gathered
home Level·both@gathered/push gathered

side entry left
side Screen right

# A choice's value is what its arms make of it (#366): `chosen` binds a vector of the first level and changes it in one
# arm. The change takes the vector the body made where it binds the name, which stands in the route's frame and not in
# the arm's, and makes it anew; the arm that does not run keeps it, so what the change makes is one value at the choice's
# moment, and the step after the choice takes that value, as it would of a vector a step made.

route signal.chosen
choice chosen/?1                                     # `if loud`
step chosen/?1/push
step chosen/list

line entry > chosen call
line Level·first > chosen value                      # `let mut both = vec![first]`, read where it is bound
line Level·second > chosen value
line Level·both@chosen/~both > chosen/?1/push value  # `both.push(second)`
line Level·second > chosen/?1/push value
line chosen/?1/push > Level·both@chosen/?1 value     # made anew in one arm, kept in the other
line Level·both@chosen/?1 > chosen/list value        # `list(&both)`
line chosen/list > Screen own

home Level·first chosen
home Level·second chosen
home Level·both@chosen/~both chosen
home Level·both@chosen/?1 chosen

side entry left
side Screen right
